.TH QUANUXCTL-SECRETS 1 "January 2026" "QuanuX" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
quanuxctl-secrets \- Manage QuanuX Credentials (OS Keyring)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quanuxctl secrets
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBsecrets\fR command group interfaces with the Operating System's Keyring (macOS Keychain, Linux Secret Service, Windows Credential Manager). It ensures that API keys and passwords are stored securely and never written to disk in plaintext.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B set \fI<KEY> <VALUE>\fR
Store a new secret or update an existing one. Replaces the value immediately.
.TP
.B get \fI<KEY>\fR
Retrieve and print the plaintext value of a secret.
.RS
.B Note:
Use this sparingly. This command is primarily for debugging or for injecting secrets into other scripts via subshells (e.g. \fIexport KEY=$(quanuxctl secrets get ...)\fR).
.RE
.TP
.B delete \fI<KEY>\fR
Remove a secret permanently from the keyring.
.TP
.B list
List all keys known to the keyring service. For security, values are masked (e.g., \fI******\fR).
.SH KNOWN KEYS
Extensions typically look for specific keys. Common ones include:
.TP
.B QUANUX_RITHMIC_USER
Rithmic Username.
.TP
.B QUANUX_RITHMIC_PASS
Rithmic Password.
.TP
.B QUANUX_FIGMA_TOKEN
Figma Personal Access Token (for Design Skill).
.SH EXAMPLES
.B 1. Store Credentials
.nf
$ quanuxctl secrets set QUANUX_RITHMIC_USER "duncan"
.fi
.P
.B 2. Inject into environment
.nf
$ export RITHMIC_USER=$(quanuxctl secrets get QUANUX_RITHMIC_USER)
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quanuxctl (1),
.BR quanuxctl-ext (1)
